Alternatively, using De Morgan's laws, ¬P ∨ ¬Q is equivalent to ¬(P ∧ Q). So, another way to express it is "It is not the case that both P and Q are true." That might be another equivalent statement.

Let me think if there are any other equivalent forms. Sometimes, people use the biconditional or other connectives, but in this case, since it's a simple implication, the main equivalents are the contrapositive and the disjunctive form.

