Okay, so I have this problem about five towns connected by roads, and each pair of towns is connected by exactly one road. I need to figure out how many ways there are to make all the roads one-way such that it's still possible to get from any town to any other town using these roads. That means the resulting directed graph should be strongly connected.

Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem better. We have five towns, so the number of roads is the number of pairs of towns, which is C(5,2) = 10 roads. Each road can be made one-way in two directions, so without any restrictions, the total number of possible one-way assignments would be 2^10 = 1024. But we have the restriction that the resulting directed graph must be strongly connected, meaning there's a directed path from any town to any other town.

I remember that in graph theory, a strongly connected directed graph is also called a strongly connected digraph. So, I need to count the number of strongly connected digraphs on five vertices where each pair of vertices is connected by exactly one directed edge. These are called tournaments in graph theory. So, the problem is essentially asking for the number of strongly connected tournaments on five vertices.

Wait, tournaments are complete oriented graphs, meaning every pair of vertices is connected by a single directed edge. So, yes, this is exactly the case here. So, the problem reduces to finding the number of strongly connected tournaments on five vertices.

I think there's a formula or a known result for the number of strongly connected tournaments. Let me recall. I remember that the number of strongly connected tournaments on n vertices can be calculated using inclusion-exclusion, considering all possible subsets of vertices and ensuring that the graph doesn't have a subset that is disconnected.

Alternatively, I think there's a recursive formula for the number of strongly connected tournaments. Let me try to recall. The total number of tournaments on n vertices is 2^(C(n,2)). The number of strongly connected tournaments can be found by subtracting the number of tournaments that are not strongly connected. A tournament is not strongly connected if it can be partitioned into two non-empty subsets where all edges go from one subset to the other, but not vice versa.

So, using inclusion-exclusion, the number of strongly connected tournaments S(n) can be calculated as:

S(n) = Total tournaments - Sum over k=1 to n-1 of [C(n-1, k-1) * S(k) * 2^(k*(n-k))]

Wait, that seems a bit complicated. Let me see if I can find a better way or if there's a known sequence for this.

I think the number of strongly connected tournaments is a known integer sequence. Let me try to recall the values for small n.

