So, how many cyclic tournaments are there on three vertices? There are two: one where the cycle is A->B->C->A, and another where the cycle is A->C->B->A. Wait, no, actually, in a tournament, the direction of the edges is determined by the outcomes. So, for three vertices, the number of cyclic tournaments is 2. Because you can have either A beats B, B beats C, and C beats A, forming a cycle, or A beats C, C beats B, and B beats A, forming another cycle. So, S(3)=2.

