None of these \( p \) values are perfect sixth powers. The closest is \( p = 1 \) when \( k^3 =4 \), but 4 isn't a cube.

Similarly for \( q \).

Therefore, it's unlikely that \( p \) and \( q \) are perfect sixth powers. Hence, \( (k^3 - 3)(m^3 - 3) \) being a perfect sixth power is very restrictive, possibly only possible if both \( p \) and \( q \) are ±1, but that would require \( k^3 =4 \) or \( m^3=4 \), which isn't possible since 4 isn't a cube.

